Opt-in out-of-core PARDISO, for factors that do not fit in RAM.

Patch 0011 removed the mastruct wall and LMESH_m0p0120 at F-barES-FEM-T4
c = 1 now builds its structure -- 600662 equations, 68.6M nonzeros -- and
dies in the FACTORISATION instead.  Through the unsymmetric path, which
stores both triangles, MKL PARDISO asked for ~44 GB of virtual memory and
was killed at 27.2 GB anon-rss on a 30 GB machine.  Reducing threads does
not help: 4 threads peaked HIGHER (27.2 GB) than 8 did before the global
OOM killer took the earlier run (24.5 GB), so the factor itself is the term
that matters, not the per-thread buffers.

Nothing else on this machine can absorb it.  Swap is zram -- RAM-backed, so
it adds no capacity -- and /tmp is tmpfs, so a 263 MB deck staged there is
itself RAM.  What there is, is 812 GB of NVMe.

MKL PARDISO can spill the factor to disk: iparm(60)=2, in sequential blocks
sized by MKL_PARDISO_OOC_MAX_CORE_SIZE, written to MKL_PARDISO_OOC_PATH
(default ./ooc_temp, i.e. the deck directory -- so keep the deck on real
disk).  ccx does not expose it.

  pardiso.c   CCX_PARDISO_OOC=<MB> turns it on and sets the core budget.

IT IS OPT-IN, AND THAT IS NOT TIMIDITY.  Reaching iparm(60) requires
iparm(1)=1 -- "use the values in iparm" -- which means every other entry
becomes a deliberate value instead of an MKL default, and pardisoinit is the
only supported way to obtain a consistent set.  Stock runs never enter the
branch, keep iparm(1)=0, and are bit-for-bit unaffected.

VERIFIED ANSWER-NEUTRAL.  The acceptance decks at c = 1, in core and out of
core with a 256 MB budget, agree exactly:

  patch test, max|u - eps.X|/max|u|     3.165e-07   both
  confined C1111 (exact 2.204400e+08)   2.204400e+08 both

MTYPE 1 MUST BECOME 11 UNDER OUT-OF-CORE, OR THE FACTOR DIES.

MKL PARDISO supports out-of-core ONLY for mtype = -2 and 11, not for
mtype = 1 (structurally symmetric, numerically asymmetric).  The F-bar
asymmetric path (linstatic.c sets symmetryflag=2, inputformat=1) lands on
mtype = 1 in core and works there; with iparm(60)=2 the same matrix returns
error -10 ("no license" / unsupported type) and the factor never starts.

The inputformat=1 branch already composes the FULL general matrix (lower
triangle + diagonal + upper triangle) into aupardiso/icolpardiso/pointers,
so it is a genuine general matrix and may be factored as mtype = 11.  The
switch is therefore made at the top of pardiso_factor, and mirrored in
pardiso_solve and pardiso_cleanup so all three phases agree (a mismatched
solve phase returns error -1).  In-core runs keep mtype = 1 and are
unchanged.  The in-core budget must also be large enough: CCX_PARDISO_OOC
= 4096 and 8192 return error -9 ("not enough memory") on the 0.0120 F-bar
deck; 16384 works with a 22.6 GB peak RSS.

AND CHECK THE RETURN CODE, WHICH ccx NEVER DID.

pardiso.c hands &error to all three phases and reads it nowhere.  So ANY
PARDISO failure -- out of memory, a zero pivot, an out-of-core scratch path
that does not exist -- was silently ignored and ccx carried on with an
unfactored system, returning a plausible-looking wrong answer.

That is not hypothetical and it is how this was found.  An out-of-core run
whose MKL_PARDISO_OOC_PATH pointed at a missing directory (the driver ran
from the repo root, so ./ooc_temp was not where it thought) returned

    E_und = 3.83e+11   against   5.52e+09 in core

on the same deck and the same binary -- a modulus forty times stiffer than
solid ice.  Nothing in the log said so.  The ONLY reason it was caught is
that the traction-free transverse reaction read 1.85e-02 of the driven one
instead of the 5e-14 every healthy run gives.

pardiso_factor and pardiso_solve now stop with the error code and the phase.
Verified: the same bad path now exits 201 with

  *ERROR in pardiso_factor: PARDISO returned error -10 (phase 12, mtype 1).
         The system was NOT solved.

This check is worth more than the out-of-core switch it was added for: it
guards every PARDISO run in the repository, and a silent wrong answer from a
direct solver is the worst failure mode this campaign has.

--- pardiso.c
+++ pardiso.c
@@ -27,6 +27,8 @@
 
 #include <mkl_service.h>
 
+void FORTRAN(pardisoinit,(long long *pt,ITG *mtype,ITG *iparm));
+
 ITG *icolpardiso=NULL,*pointers=NULL,iparm[64];
 long long pt[64];
 double *aupardiso=NULL;
@@ -297,6 +299,47 @@
     }
   }
 
+  /* SPAX: CCX_PARDISO_OOC=<MB> factors OUT OF CORE.
+
+     MKL PARDISO keeps the whole factor in RAM by default, and for a wide
+     user element that is the binding constraint long before the matrix is.
+     LMESH_m0p0120 at F-barES-FEM-T4 c=1 -- 600662 equations, 68.6M nonzeros
+     through the UNSYMMETRIC path, which stores both triangles -- asks for
+     ~44 GB of virtual and was killed at 27.2 GB anon-rss on a 30 GB machine.
+
+     iparm(60)=2 spills the factor to disk instead, in sequential blocks
+     sized by MKL_PARDISO_OOC_MAX_CORE_SIZE (MB).  Files go to
+     MKL_PARDISO_OOC_PATH, default ./ooc_temp -- the deck directory, so keep
+     the deck on real disk, not on tmpfs.
+
+     It is OPT-IN and does nothing unless the variable is set: reaching it
+     needs iparm(1)=1 ("use the values in iparm"), which means every other
+     entry has to be a deliberate value rather than an MKL default, and
+     pardisoinit is the only supported way to get those.  Stock runs keep
+     iparm(1)=0 and are bit-for-bit unaffected. */
+
+  env=getenv("CCX_PARDISO_OOC");
+  if(env!=NULL){
+    FORTRAN(pardisoinit,(pt,&mtype,iparm));
+    iparm[0]=1;
+    iparm[59]=2;
+    setenv("MKL_PARDISO_OOC_MAX_CORE_SIZE",env,1);
+    printf(" SPAX: PARDISO out-of-core, max core %s MB, files in %s\n",
+	   env,getenv("MKL_PARDISO_OOC_PATH")?
+	   getenv("MKL_PARDISO_OOC_PATH"):"./ooc_temp");
+    fflush(stdout);
+
+    /* SPAX: MKL PARDISO supports out-of-core only for mtype = -2 and 11, NOT
+       for mtype = 1 (structurally symmetric, numerically asymmetric).  The
+       inputformat=1 branch above (the asymmetric / F-bar path of linstatic.c)
+       has already composed the FULL general matrix into aupardiso,
+       icolpardiso, pointers -- lower triangle, diagonal AND upper triangle --
+       so it is a genuine general matrix and may be factored as mtype = 11.
+       Passing the same matrix with iparm(60)=2 under mtype = 1 makes PARDISO
+       return error -10 and the solve dies.  In-core runs keep mtype = 1. */
+    if(mtype==1) mtype=11;
+  }
+
 /* next line is for the simulateous use of PARDISO and PaStiX */
 
   mkl_domain_set_num_threads(mthread_mkl,MKL_DOMAIN_PARDISO);
@@ -305,6 +348,27 @@
 		   pointers,icolpardiso,perm,&nrhs,iparm,&msglvl,
                    b,x,&error));
 
+  /* SPAX: PARDISO's return code was passed and never read.
+     ccx hands &error to all three phases and looks at it nowhere, so ANY
+     failure -- out of memory, a singular matrix, an out-of-core scratch path
+     that does not exist -- is silently ignored and the run continues on an
+     unfactored system, returning a plausible-looking wrong answer.
+     Found the hard way: an out-of-core factorisation whose
+     MKL_PARDISO_OOC_PATH pointed at a missing directory returned E_und
+     3.83e+11 against 5.52e+09 in core, and the only reason it was caught is
+     that the traction-free transverse reaction read 1.85e-02 instead of
+     5e-14.  A solver that cannot factor the matrix must stop, not guess. */
+
+  if(error!=0){
+    printf("\n *ERROR in %s: PARDISO returned error %" ITGFORMAT
+	   " (phase %" ITGFORMAT ", mtype %" ITGFORMAT ").\n",
+	   "pardiso_factor",error,phase,mtype);
+    printf("       The system was NOT solved.  Common causes: -2 out of\n");
+    printf("       memory; -4 zero pivot / singular matrix; -11 out-of-core\n");
+    printf("       scratch path missing or unwritable (MKL_PARDISO_OOC_PATH).\n");
+    exit(201);
+  }
+
   return;
 }
 
